# Core Development Principles

## Code Quality & Architecture
- Write clean, maintainable, and scalable code
- Follow SOLID principles and avoid over-abstraction
- Prioritize composition over inheritance
- Use dependency injection patterns appropriately
- Prevent circular dependencies using internal module patterns
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isLoading)

## Documentation & Communication
- Use JSDoc for all public APIs and complex functionality
- Document architectural decisions and trade-offs
- Include practical examples for complex functionality
- Write all comments and documentation in English
- Provide clear, concise explanations with actionable advice

---

# TypeScript Excellence

## Type Safety & Design
- Avoid `any`; prefer `unknown` for uncertain types
- Use runtime type validation (Zod) for external data and environment variables
- Leverage TypeScript's strict mode features
- Preserve type information through transformations
- Maintain `bigint` types from external APIs
- Use generic types effectively
- Prefer types over interfaces; avoid enums, use maps

## Code Organization
- File structure: Exported component, subcomponents, helpers, static content, types
- Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., components/auth-wizard)
- Export public APIs through index files (`external.ts` for libraries)
- Favor named exports for components
- Use the Receive an Object, Return an Object (RORO) pattern

## Error Handling & Validation
- Handle errors and edge cases at the beginning of functions
- Use early returns for error conditions to avoid deeply nested if statements
- Place the happy path last in the function for improved readability
- Avoid unnecessary else statements; use if-return pattern instead
- Use guard clauses to handle preconditions and invalid states early
- Create descriptive custom error classes
- Implement proper error logging and user-friendly error messages
- Consider using custom error types or error factories for consistent error handling

---

# React & Next.js Best Practices

## Component Development
- Use functional components with TypeScript types
- Use declarative JSX and semantic HTML
- Abstract complex logic from render functions
- Keep render methods simple and declarative
- Split complex components into smaller, focused ones
- Use hooks for side effects and state management

## Import Order & Code Style
1. React imports
2. Third-party libraries  
3. Internal files
- Use "function" keyword for pure functions; omit semicolons
- Avoid unnecessary curly braces in conditional statements
- For single-line statements in conditionals, omit curly braces
- Use concise, multi-line syntax with brackets for all conditional statements

## Conditional Rendering & JSX
- Don't use ternary conditionals (condition ? trueValue : falseValue) in JSX
- Use the logical AND (&&) operator for cleaner conditional rendering
- Insert break lines strategically in JSX to improve readability
- Place static content and types at file end
- Use content variables for static content outside render functions

## Performance & Optimization
- Implement proper memoization (`useMemo`, `useCallback`)
- Optimize re-renders and critical rendering paths
- Lazy load components when appropriate
- Optimize images: WebP format, size data, lazy loading (under 250kB)
- Use dynamic loading for non-critical components
- Minimize 'use client', 'useEffect', and 'setState'; favor RSC
- Wrap client components in Suspense with fallback

## State Management
- Rely on React's Component Tree for State Changes (useState, useReducer, useContext)
- Use appropriate state management solutions
- Avoid unnecessary state
- Keep state as local as possible
- Use context API appropriately

---

# Styling & Design

## CSS & Styling Approach
- Use custom CSS for components and styling; never use inline styles
- Use mobile-first approach for responsive design
- Use CSS-in-JS or external stylesheets
- Follow team styling conventions and design system tokens when available
- Use CSS modules or styled-components
- Follow BEM or similar naming conventions
- Keep styles modular and reusable
- ALWAYS create a div with className="container" that wraps every component

## Responsive Design
- Implement responsive design patterns
- Maintain consistent spacing and formatting
- Prioritize Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, FID)

---

# Architecture Patterns

## Services Layer
- Encapsulate business logic clearly
- Use dependency injection patterns
- Follow consistent naming conventions (e.g., `UserService`)
- Keep services stateless when possible
- Code in services/ dir always throw user-friendly errors that tanStackQuery can catch and show to the user

## Data Layer & API Design
- Use appropriate data fetching patterns
- Implement proper caching strategies
- Handle loading and error states properly
- Validate data at boundaries
- Design clear, intuitive interfaces
- Maintain backward compatibility when possible

## Testing Strategy
- Write descriptive, focused tests
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Use appropriate mocking strategies
- Maintain good test coverage

---

# Security & Performance

## Security Considerations
- Validate and sanitize user inputs
- Implement proper authentication patterns
- Follow security best practices for data handling
- Avoid common vulnerabilities (XSS, CSRF, etc.)

## Performance Guidelines
- Optimize critical rendering paths
- Use appropriate caching strategies
- Minimize bundle sizes and 'use client' usage
- Implement efficient data structures
- Consider memory usage patterns

